<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Albert Eckhout</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1610–<a href="/years/1665">1665</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Albert Eckhout was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> painter from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groningen">Groningen</a> who worked in Dutch <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> from 1637 to 1644 in the household of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johan-maurits-of-nassau-siegen">Johan Maurits of Nassau-Siegen</a>. He painted life-size portraits of Indigenous, African and mixed-descent inhabitants of Brazil, as well as still lifes of tropical fruit, among the earliest European images of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> made from life. Many of his works are in the National Museum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/albert-eckhout.jpg" alt="Albert Eckhout" width="320" height="362" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Albert Eckhout · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Albert_Eckhout_(atrib.)_-_Cabe%C3%A7a_de_menino,_1637.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
